      "content": "## Problem Statement\n\n### Operational Context\n\nA utility company operates a 10-megawatt solar farm. Operators collected hourly data over several days, recording the solar irradiance (incoming sunlight power in watts per square meter) and the corresponding energy output (in kilowatt-hours) from the panel array.\n\nSolar irradiance depends on time of day, cloud cover, atmospheric aerosols, and seasonal sun angle. Energy output depends on irradiance plus system inefficiencies: inverter losses, temperature derating, soiling (dust on panels), and wiring losses.\n\n### Dataset: Solar Farm Hourly Observations\n\n| Hour | Irradiance (W/m^2) | Output (kWh) |\n|:-----|:-------------------|:-------------|\n| 1 | 120 | 105 |\n| 2 | 250 | 232 |\n| 3 | 380 | 358 |\n| 4 | 520 | 495 |\n| 5 | 680 | 648 |\n| 6 | 800 | 765 |\n| 7 | 920 | 882 |\n| 8 | 1000 | 962 |\n| 9 | 850 | 815 |\n| 10 | 620 | 590 |\n| 11 | 400 | 380 |\n| 12 | 180 | 165 |\n\n**Operational Note:** The relationship is nearly linear but not perfect. Inverter clipping at high irradiance, temperature-related efficiency losses at midday, and dust accumulation introduce non-linearities. The linear model captures the dominant pattern while residual error reflects these secondary effects."
