    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Learning Rate Analysis: The Data-Scale Lesson\n\n### Why Alpha Must Match Data Scale\n\nThe most important lesson from this lab: **learning rate is data-dependent.**\n\nIn the original housing lab, features ranged 1-2, and alpha=0.01 worked well. Here, irradiance ranges 120-1000. The gradient includes error * x[i], so gradients are ~500x larger. Using alpha=0.01 here would cause immediate catastrophic divergence.\n\n### Three Regimes\n\n1. **Too small (alpha=1e-8):** Converges but takes 10x more iterations. In real-time grid operations, slow training delays deployment of updated forecasts.\n\n2. **Just right (alpha=5e-7):** Smooth monotonic convergence. Cost drops rapidly then plateaus.\n\n3. **Too large (alpha=1e-5):** Parameters oscillate with growing amplitude. Cost explodes. Model produces nonsensical predictions like negative energy output or 10x overproduction.\n\n### Practical Implication for Energy Systems\n\nProduction ML pipelines for energy forecasting use techniques like feature normalization (scaling all inputs to similar ranges) to make learning rates transferable across datasets. Understanding WHY normalization helps requires understanding this data-scale dependency."
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "source": "# LEARNING RATE COMPARISON\n\nprint(\"LEARNING RATE ANALYSIS: DATA-SCALE SENSITIVITY\")\nprint(\"=\" * 75)\n\nalphas = [\n    (1e-8, 'Too Small (slow convergence)'),\n    (5e-7, 'Just Right (smooth convergence)'),\n    (2e-6, 'Borderline (some oscillation)'),\n    (1e-5, 'Too Large (divergence)'),\n]\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(16, 12))\n\nfor idx, (alpha_val, desc) in enumerate(alphas):\n    ax = axes[idx // 2][idx % 2]\n    \n    w_t, b_t, J_t, p_t = gradient_descent(\n        x_train, y_train, 0.0, 0.0, alpha_val, 500, compute_cost, compute_gradient\n    )\n    \n    if J_t[-1] < 1e15:\n        ax.plot(range(len(J_t)), J_t, 'b-', linewidth=2)\n        ax.axhline(y=cost_opt, color='green', linestyle='--', alpha=0.5, label=f'Optimal J={cost_opt:.2f}')\n    else:\n        ax.plot(range(len(J_t)), np.log10(np.array(J_t) + 1), 'r-', linewidth=2)\n        ax.set_ylabel('log10(Cost)')\n    \n    converged = 'CONVERGED' if J_t[-1] < cost_opt * 2 else 'DIVERGED'\n    color = 'green' if J_t[-1] < cost_opt * 2 else 'red'\n    \n    ax.set_title(f'alpha={alpha_val:.1e} ({desc})\\nFinal J={J_t[-1]:.2e} [{converged}]',\n                fontsize=10, fontweight='bold', color=color)\n    ax.set_xlabel('Iteration')\n    if idx < 2:\n        ax.set_ylabel('Cost J(w,b)')\n    ax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\n    ax.legend(fontsize=8)\n    \n    print(f\"alpha={alpha_val:.1e} ({desc}): Final J={J_t[-1]:.2e}, w={w_t:.6f}, b={b_t:.2f} [{converged}]\")\n\nplt.suptitle('Learning Rate Impact: Data Scale Matters', fontsize=14, fontweight='bold', y=0.98)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nprint(\"\\nKey lesson: The optimal alpha for this data is ~5e-7, vastly smaller\")\nprint(\"than the 0.01 used for housing data. This is because irradiance values\")\nprint(\"(~100s) are much larger than house sizes (1-2), producing larger gradients.\")\nprint(\"\\nIn production: Feature normalization (scaling inputs to 0-1 range)\")\nprint(\"makes learning rates transferable across different datasets.\")",
      "comment": "Four-panel learning rate comparison. The 'too small' alpha barely decreases cost in 500 iterations. The 'just right' alpha converges smoothly. The 'too large' alpha diverges explosively. The log-scale fallback handles overflow. This comparison directly demonstrates why learning rate selection is data-dependent, a key practical lesson for ML practitioners."
